'''Regina Inez Hinojosa''' ({{IPAc-en|ˌ|iː|n|ə|ˈ|h|oʊ|s|ə}}; {{respell|EE|noh|HOH|sə}}; born December 8, 1973)<ref>{{cite web |title=Rep. Gina Hinojosa |url=https://www.txdirectory.com/online/person/?id=56162&office=16795 |website=Texas State Directory Online |language=en-US |access-date=July 24, 2026}}</ref> is an American lawyer and politician who is the [[Democratic Party (United States)|Democratic]] nominee for the [[2026 Texas gubernatorial election]]. Hinojosa is a member of the [[Texas House of Representatives]], representing the [[Texas's 49th House of Representatives district|49th district]]. She is a member of the Democratic Party and was sworn into the Texas House on January 10, 2017, after winning the November 2016 general election.<ref>{{cite news |last1=Eaton |first1=Tim |date=March 2, 2016 |title=Gina Hinojosa declares victory for House District 49 |url=https://www.statesman.com/story/news/2016/08/16/gina-hinojosa-declares-victory-for-house-district-49/9835700007/ |website=Austin American-Statesman |language=en-US |access-date=January 13, 2017 |archive-date=January 16, 2017|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20170116153703/http://www.statesman.com/news/gina-hinojosa-declares-victory-for-house-district/fDEi35iAd2JX7Wwh9Csr1M/ |url-status=live}}</ref><ref>{{cite news |last1=Lopez |first1=Ashley |title=Gina Hinojosa Prepares to Tackle School Funding, Local Control in Her First Legislative Session |url=http://kut.org/post/gina-hinojosa-prepares-tackle-school-funding-local-control-her-first-legislative-session |website=KUT |language=en-US |date=January 2, 2017 |access-date=January 13, 2017}}</ref>

Before becoming a state legislator, Hinojosa worked for the [[American Federation of State, County and Municipal Employees]] and for the law firm of Kator, Parks & Weiser. In 2005, she was part of the legal team that sued [[US house majority leader|U.S. House Majority Leader]] [[Tom DeLay]]. She was also a member of the [[Austin Independent School District]]'s school board and has worked part-time for [[Catholic Charities USA]], Texas Rural Legal Aid, and the Equal Justice Center.<ref>{{cite news |last1=Whittaker |first1=Richard |date=February 5, 2016 |title=Meet the Candidate: Gina Hinojosa |url=http://www.austinchronicle.com/news/2016-02-05/meet-the-candidate-gina-hinojosa/ |website=The Austin Chronicle |language=en-US |access-date=January 13, 2017}}</ref>

On October 15, 2025, Hinojosa announced her candidacy for [[Governor of Texas|governor]] in the 2026 Texas gubernatorial election.<ref name="Guo 2025">{{Cite web |last=Guo |first=Kayla |date=October 15, 2025 |title=Texas Rep. Gina Hinojosa joins Democratic race to challenge Gov. Greg Abbott |url=https://www.texastribune.org/2025/10/15/gina-hinojosa-texas-governor-campaign-launch-2026-greg-abbott/ |website=The Texas Tribune |language=en-US |access-date=October 15, 2025}}</ref> Hinojosa won the Democratic primary on March 3, 2026, and will face incumbent [[Republican Party (United States)|Republican]] governor [[Greg Abbott]] in the general election.<ref name="Guo 2025"/><ref>{{Cite web |title=Texas Governor Primary Results 2026 |url=https://www.nbcnews.com/politics/2026-primary-elections/texas-governor-results/ |work=NBC News |language=en-US |date=March 3, 2026 |access-date=March 4, 2026}}</ref>

== Early life, education, and career ==
[[File:Gina Hinojosa at the State Democratic Convention.jpg|thumb|Hinojosa speaking at the [[Texas Democratic Party]]'s 2014 convention.]]
Regina Inez Hinojosa was born in [[McAllen, Texas]], and is the daughter of politician [[Gilberto Hinojosa]]. She grew up in [[Brownsville, Texas|Brownsville]] and spent summers with her grandparents in [[Mission, Texas|Mission]]. After graduating from [[Homer Hanna High School]] in 1992, she attended the [[University of Texas at Austin]] as a Plan II Honors/Government major, graduating with her [[Bachelor of Arts]] in 1996. From there she earned her [[Juris Doctor]] degree from [[George Washington University Law School]] in 1999.<ref name="Jimenez" /> She spent one semester at the [[University of Texas School of Law]] where she met her husband.<ref>{{Cite journal |last=Hinojosa |first=Gina |date=September 1, 2022 |title=Opening Keynote |url=https://search.ebscohost.com/login.aspx?direct=true&db=a9h&AN=161636799&site=eds-live&scope=site |journal=Texas Hispanic Journal of Law & Policy |volume=28 |issue=1 |page=103 |issn=1547-4887 |url-access=subscription |via=EBSCOhost}}</ref>

Hinojosa started her career representing union members as a civil rights lawyer. In 2012, facing [[Austin Independent School District]]'s closing of schools, including her son's school, Pease Elementary, she joined the Austin ISD school board. She served four years until 2016, when she ran for [[Texas's 49th House of Representatives district]], where she served five terms.<ref name="Jimenez">{{Cite web |last=Jimenez |first=Francisco E. |date=October 24, 2025 |title=Democrat cites Valley roots in bid to challenge Republican Gov. Greg Abbott |url=https://myrgv.com/local-news/2025/10/24/democrat-cites-valley-roots-in-bid-to-challenge-republican-gov-greg-abbott/ |website=myRGV.com |language=en-US |access-date=July 24, 2026}}</ref><ref>{{Cite web |date=2018 |title=Representative Gina Hinojosa |url=https://www.nacogdoches.org/forms/Hinojosa_bio.pdf |website=Nacogdoches County Chamber of Commerce |language=en-US |access-date=July 24, 2026}}</ref>

== Texas House of Representatives ==
=== 2017 ===
In the [[85th Texas Legislature]], Hinojosa proposed directing $3 million to the Texas Health and Human Services Commission to help [[Human trafficking in Texas|trafficking victims]] receive necessary services.<ref>{{Cite news |last=Walters |first=Edgar |date=April 10, 2017 |title=House lawmakers take a stab at funding for trafficking victims |url=https://www.texastribune.org/2017/04/10/house-lawmakers-take-stab-funding-trafficking-victims/ |work=The Texas Tribune |language=en-US |access-date=July 24, 2026}}</ref> The proposal passed the House 113 to 32, but was stripped from the budget during negotiations with the Senate.<ref>{{Cite news |last=Walters |first=Edgar |date=May 25, 2017 |title=Behind closed doors, Texas lawmakers strip funding for sex trafficking victims |url=https://www.texastribune.org/2017/05/25/behind-closed-doors-texas-lawmakers-strip-funding-trafficking-victims/ |work=The Texas Tribune |language=en-US |access-date=July 24, 2026}}</ref>

=== 2019 ===
At an organizing campaign for the Healthy Texas Act of 2019, Hinojosa pledged to introduce legislation to provide every Texas resident with comprehensive healthcare services.<ref>{{Cite news |last=King |first=Michael |date=December 6, 2018 |title=Dems Call for Health Care for All Texans |url=https://www.austinchronicle.com/news/dems-call-for-health-care-for-all-texans-11774228/ |work=The Austin Chronicle |language=en-US |access-date=July 24, 2026}}</ref> The bill was considered in committee, but did not move forward.<ref>{{Cite web |title=HB 4127 |url=https://capitol.texas.gov/BillLookup/History.aspx?LegSess=86R&Bill=HB4127 |website=Texas Legislature Online |language=en-US |access-date=July 24, 2026}}</ref> During the [[86th Texas Legislature]], Hinojosa also authored HB 1307, which established a system allowing individuals impacted by disasters to apply for assistance. The bill was signed into law.<ref>{{Cite web |title=HB 1307 |url=https://capitol.texas.gov/BillLookup/History.aspx?LegSess=86R&Bill=HB1307}}</ref>

=== 2021 ===
During the [[87th Texas Legislature]], Hinojosa filed House Bill 73, which would have prohibited the use of the [[Gay panic defense|gay/trans panic defense]]. Under the panic defense, criminal penalties for assault and murder can be lessened or eliminated if the perpetrator claims they lost control and acted violently in response to the victim's sexual orientation or gender identity. The bill was rejected by the House Committee on Criminal Jurisprudence.<ref>{{Cite news |last=Brown |first=Sadie |date=June 18, 2021 |title='The Great State of Hate': Texas Declines to Ban 'Gay/Trans Panic' Defense |url=https://www.texasobserver.org/the-great-state-of-hate-texas-declines-to-ban-gay-trans-panic-defense/  |work=Texas Observer |language=en-US |access-date=July 24, 2026}}</ref>

In July 2021, Democratic members of the Texas House, including Hinojosa, left the state in an effort to prevent the passage of [[Republican efforts to restrict voting following the 2020 United States presidential election|voting restriction legislation]], which would ban drive-thru and 24-hour voting, prohibit local election officials from distributing unrequested mail-in ballot applications, and grant partisan poll watchers unrestricted movement at voting sites, among other changes.<ref>{{Cite news |last=DeBenedetto |first=Paul |date=July 12, 2021 |title=Texas Democrats Leave The State In An Effort To Block GOP Voting Restrictions |url=https://www.npr.org/2021/07/12/1015315950/texas-democrats-leave-state-in-effort-to-block-gop-voting-restrictions |work=NPR |language=en-US |access-date=July 24, 2026}}</ref><ref>{{Cite news |last=Kroll |first=Andy |date=July 13, 2021 |title=A Texas Democrat Explains Why She's Fled the State — and What's Next for Voting Rights |url=https://www.rollingstone.com/politics/politics-features/texas-democrat-walkout-voting-rights-congress-1196818/ |work=Rolling Stone |language=en-US |access-date=July 24, 2026}}</ref> Although the bill eventually passed, provisions that would have made it easier for judges to overturn election results and prohibited early morning voting on Sundays (potentially impeding Black churches' post-service voting tradition) were eliminated.<ref>{{Cite news |last=Ura |first=Alexa |date=September 7, 2021 |title=Gov. Greg Abbott signs Texas voting bill into law, overcoming Democratic quorum breaks |url=https://www.texastribune.org/2021/09/07/texas-voting-bill-greg-abbott/ |work=The Texas Tribune |language=en-US |access-date=July 24, 2026}}</ref>

=== 2023 ===
During the [[88th Texas Legislature]], Hinojosa authored the Fully Fund Our Future Act. The bill would have invested 40 billion dollars into the Texas public school system, providing teachers with a $15,000 raise and school support staff with a $5,500 raise, increasing per-student funding from $6,160 to $8,947, and allocating an additional $2 billion for special education services.<ref>{{Cite news |last=Basco |first=Isabella |date=October 19, 2023 |title=Texas Democrats look to pump $40 billion into public school system with school finance bill |url=https://www.kvue.com/article/news/politics/texas-legislature/texas-school-funding-bill-fully-fund-out-future-act-hb-177/269-4b050592-f3aa-434f-b963-7ee0db395c8d |work=KVUE}}</ref><ref>{{Cite web |title=HB 177 |url=https://capitol.texas.gov/BillLookup/History.aspx?LegSess=883&Bill=HB177}}</ref> However, Texas Governor [[Greg Abbott]] stated that he would not permit increasing public education funding or providing teacher pay raises unless [[school voucher]] legislation passed.<ref>{{Cite news |last=Pandey |first=Maia |date=October 12, 2023 |title=Gov. Greg Abbott says he'll add teacher pay to the special session — but only if lawmakers pass school voucher bill |url=https://www.texastribune.org/2023/10/12/greg-abbott-school-vouchers-teacher-raises/ }}</ref> As a result, public school districts did not receive additional state funding in 2023.<ref>{{Cite news |last=Svitek |first=Patrick |last2=Despart |first2=Zach |last3=Lopez |first3=Brian |date=December 22, 2023 |title=How Gov. Greg Abbott lost a yearlong fight to create school vouchers |url=https://www.texastribune.org/2023/12/22/texas-school-vouchers-greg-abbott/ |work=The Texas Tribune |language=en-US |access-date=July 24, 2026}}</ref>

=== 2025 ===
In February 2025, Hinojosa posted a link to an interactive website where users could learn how much money local school districts would lose as a result of school voucher legislation. Governor Abbott, who had designated the passage of school vouchers as his first priority of the session, stated that Democrats either don't understand the legislation "or they lie about it." Hinojosa responded, "Call me a liar to my face," in a post accompanied by a video offering to educate the governor on school finance.<ref>{{Cite news |last=Moritz |first=John |date=February 13, 2025 |title='Call me a liar to my face': Texas Gov. Abbott, Rep. Hinojosa spar over school vouchers |url=https://www.statesman.com/story/news/politics/state/2025/02/13/school-choice-vouchers-texas-gov-greg-abbott-rep-gina-hinojosa-social-media-feud/78541100007/ |work=Austin-American Statesman |language=en-US |access-date=July 24, 2026}}</ref>

In August 2025, Hinojosa was one of the 51 Democratic members of the Texas House who left the state to participate in the [[Quorum-busting|quorum bust]] in order to delay the passage of [[2025 Texas redistricting|new congressional maps]].<ref>{{Cite web |last=McCracken |first=Ford |last2=McKim |first2=Dylan |last3=Neas |first3=Cora |date=August 5, 2025 |title=Here are the Texas House Democrats who broke quorum |url=https://thehill.com/homenews/5435394-here-are-the-texas-house-democrats-who-broke-quorum/ |website=The Hill}}</ref> While she was absent from the state, Attorney General [[Ken Paxton]] filed a lawsuit to attempt to remove Hinojosa, along with a dozen other representatives, from office by declaring their seats vacant.<ref>{{Cite web |last=Klibanoff |first=Eleanor |date=2025-08-08 |title=Ken Paxton asks Texas Supreme Court to expel 13 House Democrats over redistricting standoff |url=https://www.texastribune.org/2025/08/08/ken-paxton-texas-democrats-supreme-court-removal-redistricting/ |access-date=2025-11-07 |website=The Texas Tribune |language=en-US}}</ref> The members ultimately returned to the state before the [[Supreme Court of Texas|Texas Supreme Court]] could issue a ruling,<ref>{{Cite web |last=Gainey |first=Blaise |date=2025-08-18 |title=Texas House Democrats return to Capitol after two-week walkout, will have DPS escorts |url=https://www.kut.org/politics/2025-08-18/texas-democrats-return-to-austin-redistricting-california-congressional-maps |access-date=2025-11-07 |website=KUT Radio, Austin's NPR Station |language=en}}</ref> and the maps passed through the legislature, triggering a [[2025–2026 United States redistricting|nationwide cycle of redistricting]].<ref>{{Cite news |last=Schneider |first=Andrew |date=2025-08-23 |title=Texas passes midterm redistricting sought by Trump as California plans to counter |url=https://www.npr.org/2025/08/21/nx-s1-5496659/texas-congressional-redistricting-trump |access-date=2025-11-07 |work=NPR |language=en}}</ref><ref>{{Cite web |date=2025-11-05 |title=What's next in the national redistricting fight after California approved a new U.S. House map |url=https://www.pbs.org/newshour/politics/whats-next-in-the-national-redistricting-fight-after-california-approved-a-new-u-s-house-map |access-date=2025-11-07 |website=PBS News |language=en-us|author-first1=David A.|author-last1=Lieb}}</ref> In May 2026, the Texas Supreme Court ruled that it did not have jurisdiction in the legislative dispute and dismissed the case against the absent members.<ref>{{Cite web |last=Klibanoff |first=Eleanor |last2=Guo |first2=Kayla |date=May 15, 2026 |title=Democrat Gene Wu can keep House seat, Texas Supreme Court rules |url=https://www.texastribune.org/2026/05/15/texas-supreme-court-gene-wu-greg-abbott-redistricting-map-quorum-break/ |website=The Texas Tribune |language=en-US |access-date=June 1, 2026}}</ref>

== 2026 gubernatorial campaign ==
{{main|2026 Texas gubernatorial election}}
On October 15, 2025, Hinojosa announced her candidacy for [[Governor of Texas|governor]] in the [[2026 Texas gubernatorial election|2026 election]].<ref name="Guo 2025"/> Her opponents in the [[2026 Texas gubernatorial election#Democratic primary|Democratic primary]] included former Representative and [[2006 Texas gubernatorial election|2006]] gubernatorial nominee [[Chris Bell (politician)|Chris Bell]]. On March 3, 2026, she won the primary with 59.9 percent of the vote.<ref>{{Cite web |last=Gainey |first=Blaise |date=March 3, 2026 |title=Texas Rep. Gina Hinojosa wins Democratic nomination, will face Gov. Greg Abbott in November |url=https://www.houstonpublicmedia.org/articles/news/politics/election-2026/2026/03/03/545125/texas-rep-gina-hinojosa-wins-democratic-nomination-will-face-gov-greg-abbott-in-november/ |website=Houston Public Media |language=en-US |access-date=March 7, 2026}}</ref> She will face [[Greg Abbott]] in November.

== Political positions ==

=== Education ===
Hinojosa has been a vocal opponent of the state's current academic testing regimen for children and has advocated for an end to the [[STAAR|State of Texas Assessments of Academic Readiness]] (STAAR) tests.<ref>{{Cite web |last=Kennedy |first=Jahmal |date=2025-08-25 |title=Texas House set to debate HB-8 Tuesday; bill would replace STAAR test |url=https://cbsaustin.com/news/local/texas-house-set-to-debate-hb-8-tuesday |access-date=2026-08-27 |website=KEYE |language=en}}</ref> If elected as governor, she has promised to replace the Commissioner of the [[Texas Education Agency]] [[Mike Morath]].<ref>{{Cite web |last=Prazan |first=Phil |date=2026-06-15 |title=Gina Hinojosa says she would fire Texas education commissioner if elected governor |url=https://www.nbcdfw.com/news/politics/lone-star-politics/gina-hinojosa-says-she-would-fire-texas-education-commissioner-if-elected-governor/4036769/ |access-date=2026-08-27 |website=NBC 5 Dallas-Fort Worth |language=en-US}}</ref> Hinojosa has also called for an end to the voucher program passed in the 2025 legislative session.<ref>{{Cite web |last=Kennedy |first=Jahmal |date=2026-06-08 |title=Hinojosa launches program to fight Texas school closures, citing public school crisis |url=https://cbsaustin.com/news/local/hinojosa-launches-program-to-fight-texas-school-closures-citing-public-school-crisis |access-date=2026-08-27 |website=KEYE |language=en}}</ref><ref>{{Cite web |last=Edison |first=Jaden |last2=Dey |first2=Sneha |last3=Menchaca |first3=Megan |date=2026-08-27 |title=A look at Texas gubernatorial candidates’ education priorities |url=https://www.texastribune.org/2026/08/27/texas-governor-race-abbott-hinojosa-education/ |access-date=2026-08-27 |website=The Texas Tribune |language=en-US}}</ref> In September 2026, she expressed her opposition to the widespread use of [[Artificial intelligence|AI]] tools in education for young children.<ref>{{Cite web |last=Mizan |first=Nusaiba |date=2026-09-22 |title=Gina Hinojosa blasts HISD's AI expansion as Greg Abbott's 'experiment' on kids |url=https://www.houstonchronicle.com/news/houston-texas/education/hisd/article/future-2-ai-schools-governor-race-22442481.php |access-date=2026-09-24 |website=Houston Chronicle |language=en}}</ref>

=== Data centers ===
Hinojosa has called for a moratorium on the construction of data centers in Texas.<ref>{{cite web |last1=Wermund |first1=Benjamin |date=July 23, 2026 |title=Gina Hinojosa calls on Greg Abbott to halt data center construction |url=https://www.houstonchronicle.com/politics/texas/article/gina-hinojosa-calls-greg-abbott-halt-data-center-22357152.php |website=Houston Chronicle |language=en-US |access-date=July 24, 2026}}</ref> She said that data centers "are owned by the richest men in the world. We're all footing the bill. There are no rules."<ref>{{cite web |last1=Levy |first1=Marc |date=July 23, 2026 |title=Democrats seize on AI data center backlash that’s dividing rural Republicans in places like Texas |url=https://apnews.com/article/data-centers-texas-ai-gina-hinojosa-abbott-3624f9a97e584c46d0ac65ca3870adcf |website=Associated Press |language=en-US |access-date=July 24, 2026}}</ref>

=== Taxation ===
Hinojosa supports the suspension of the Texas state [[Fuel taxes in the United States|gas tax]] as a way to ease [[2026 Iran war fuel crisis|rising fuel prices]] caused by the [[2026 Iran war]].<ref>{{cite web |last1=Schwager |first1=Adam |date=April 24, 2026 |title=How Texas Gubernatorial candidates plans to cut Texans’ taxes |url=https://www.kxan.com/news/texas-politics/how-texas-gubernatorial-candidates-plans-to-cut-texans-taxes/ |website=KXAN |language=en-US |access-date=July 24, 2026}}</ref> Campaigning as a candidate for governor, Hinojosa proposed sending a $1,500 rebate check to every Texas household from the state's Economic Stabilization Fund, which she called a "corruption tax refund.".<ref>{{cite web |last1=Guo |first1=Kayla |date=July 7, 2026 |title=Gina Hinojosa proposes sending every Texas household $1,500 in her bid to oust Greg Abbott |url=http://texastribune.org/2026/07/07/gina-hinojosa-proposes-sending-every-texas-household-1500-in-her-bid-to-oust-greg-abbott/ |website=The Texas Tribune |language=en-US |access-date=July 24, 2026}}</ref>

=== Immigration and border security ===
In November 2023, Hinojosa voted against Senate Bill 3, which funded border barrier construction and border security operations, and against [[Texas Senate Bill 4 (2023)|Senate Bill 4]], which created a state crime of illegal entry.<ref name="journal">{{cite web |title=House Journal, 88th Legislature, Fourth Called Session, Fifth Day |url=https://lrl.texas.gov/scanned/HouseJournals/88/11142023_5_45.PDF |website=Texas Legislative Reference Library |date=2023-11-14 |pages=46–48, 75–76 |access-date=2026-10-26}}</ref> In an October 2025 interview, she said she supports securing the border and that law-abiding immigrants with roots in their communities need "some kind of path to be here legally." She also called state spending on [[Operation Lone Star]] a misappropriation of taxpayer money.<ref name="observer">{{cite web |title='Failures and Grift': Gina Hinojosa Wants to Stop Greg Abbott from Making History |url=https://www.texasobserver.org/gina-hinojosa-campaign-for-texas-governor/ |website=[[The Texas Observer]] |date=2025-10-16 |access-date=2026-10-26}}</ref>
 
Hinojosa has criticized federal immigration enforcement in Texas, saying [[United States Immigration and Customs Enforcement|ICE]] acts with "lethal lawlessness."<ref>{{cite web |title=Can Gina Hinojosa Flip Texas? |url=https://www.austinchronicle.com/news/can-gina-hinojosa-flip-texas/ |website=[[The Austin Chronicle]] |access-date=2026-10-26}}</ref> She has called for investigations into shootings by federal immigration agents, including the March 2025 death of Ruben Ray Martinez on South Padre Island,<ref>{{cite web |title=Hinojosa accuses DPS, feds of cover-up in last year's ICE shooting death of San Antonio man |url=https://www.sacurrent.com/news/texas-news/hinojosa-accuses-dps-feds-of-coverup-in-last-years-ice-shooting-death-of-san-antonio-man/ |website=[[San Antonio Current]] |access-date=2026-10-26}}</ref> and the July 2026 death of [[Killing of Lorenzo Salgado Araujo|Lorenzo Salgado Araujo]] in Houston, after which she asked for body camera footage and an investigation involving the [[Texas Ranger Division|Texas Rangers]].<ref>{{cite web |title=STATEMENT: Hinojosa Calls for Transparency, Release of Body Cam Footage After ICE Killing of Lorenzo Salgado Araujo |url=https://ginafortexas.com/2026/07/statement-hinojosa-calls-for-transparency-release-of-body-cam-footage-after-ice-killing-of-lorenzo-salgado-araujo/ |website=Gina Hinojosa for Texas |date=2026-07-08 |access-date=2026-10-26}}</ref>

